1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,163
MyCampusView Academy.

2
00:00:03,213 --> 00:00:05,900
Robotics 101, video 8.

3
00:00:05,950 --> 00:00:09,260
Your first Arduino program.

4
00:00:10,450 --> 00:00:13,660
Now you will write your first program.

5
00:00:13,710 --> 00:00:17,177
Programs for Arduino are called sketches.

6
00:00:17,227 --> 00:00:21,380
You write them in the Arduino IDE, which is free.

7
00:00:22,750 --> 00:00:27,835
Install the Arduino IDE and plug in the board with the USB cable.

8
00:00:27,885 --> 00:00:33,129
Under Tools, choose the board Arduino Uno, and then choose the port

9
00:00:33,179 --> 00:00:34,630
your Arduino is on.

10
00:00:35,950 --> 00:00:38,643
Every sketch has two parts.

11
00:00:38,693 --> 00:00:43,112
setup runs once, when the robot switches on.

12
00:00:43,162 --> 00:00:46,668
loop runs again and again, forever.

13
00:00:46,718 --> 00:00:49,410
Remember sense, think, act?

14
00:00:49,460 --> 00:00:51,950
That happens inside loop.

15
00:00:53,250 --> 00:00:55,299
Here is a tiny program.

16
00:00:55,349 --> 00:00:58,129
pinMode makes pin 13 an output.

17
00:00:58,179 --> 00:01:03,241
In the loop, we switch the light on, wait half a second,

18
00:01:03,291 --> 00:01:05,979
switch it off, and wait again.

19
00:01:06,029 --> 00:01:09,630
Every instruction ends with a semicolon.

20
00:01:10,950 --> 00:01:13,532
Click the tick to check your code.

21
00:01:13,582 --> 00:01:17,711
If you see a red error, read it carefully: it is often

22
00:01:17,761 --> 00:01:20,111
a missing semicolon or bracket.

23
00:01:20,161 --> 00:01:24,600
Then click the arrow to upload, and watch the light blink.

24
00:01:26,100 --> 00:01:27,502
Let us recap.

25
00:01:27,552 --> 00:01:31,857
setup() runs once; loop() runs forever.

26
00:01:31,907 --> 00:01:36,324
Every instruction ends with a semicolon.

27
00:01:36,374 --> 00:01:38,558
Verify, then upload.

28
00:01:38,608 --> 00:01:42,690
Next: Reading the IR sensors in code.
